import{Ct as e,rt as t,t as n,tt as r}from"./chunks/framework.CMX8camj.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"The Documentation Pages That Wouldn't Disappear","description":"","frontmatter":{"authorship":["ai"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"developer/story/docs-preview-ghost-pages.md","filePath":"developer/story/docs-preview-ghost-pages.md"}`),a={name:`developer/story/docs-preview-ghost-pages.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,{"data-pagefind-body":!0,"data-pagefind-meta":`date:1795287477000`},[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="the-documentation-pages-that-wouldn-t-disappear" tabindex="-1">The Documentation Pages That Wouldn&#39;t Disappear <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-documentation-pages-that-wouldn-t-disappear" aria-label="Permalink to “The Documentation Pages That Wouldn&#39;t Disappear”">​</a></h1><p><em>Why publishing PR previews changed how we deploy production documentation.</em></p><p>Written with AI, November 2026. The preview implementation requires <code>nx-github-pages</code> 1.3.1; the dependency update supersedes PR #185.</p><h2 id="one-branch-two-kinds-of-content" tabindex="-1">One Branch, Two Kinds of Content <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-branch-two-kinds-of-content" aria-label="Permalink to “One Branch, Two Kinds of Content”">​</a></h2><p>Notopia publishes its documentation and API reference from the <code>gh-pages</code> branch. A production build belongs at the branch root and is served under <code>/notopia/</code>. A pull request preview belongs under <code>pr/&lt;number&gt;/</code> and is served under <code>/notopia/pr/&lt;number&gt;/</code>.</p><p>That shared branch gives us two requirements that pull in opposite directions:</p><ul><li>A production deployment must replace the old production site, including deleting pages that no longer exist in the new build.</li><li>The same deployment must leave the <code>pr/</code> directory alone, so it does not erase other people&#39;s previews.</li></ul><p>Publishing the build as a complete replacement satisfies the first requirement but loses the previews. Keeping everything from the old branch protects previews but also keeps obsolete pages.</p><h2 id="the-ghost-page" tabindex="-1">The Ghost Page <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-ghost-page" aria-label="Permalink to “The Ghost Page”">​</a></h2><p>We looked at <code>nx-github-pages</code> 1.3.1, which supports both preview deployments and scheduled preview cleanup. Its documentation recommends <code>syncWithBaseBranch</code> to preserve previews during production deployments.</p><p>The <a href="https://github.com/AgentEnder/nx-github-pages/blob/293f6bdec51acde86982c0ffb5fbc6d80e957c44/packages/nx-github-pages/src/executors/deploy/executor.ts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1.3.1 deploy executor</a> builds a fresh Git history from the output directory and merges the existing <code>gh-pages</code> branch into it. The default merge favors new files when both histories have a conflicting file. But a file present only in the old branch is not a conflict: Git can retain it in the merge. The executor then uses a force-push.</p><p>For example:</p><div class="language-text" dir="ltr"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes catppuccin-latte catppuccin-mocha" style="--shiki-light:#4c4f69;--shiki-dark:#cdd6f4;--shiki-light-bg:#eff1f5;--shiki-dark-bg:#1e1e2e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Old gh-pages:  index.html  retired-guide.html  pr/123/index.html</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>New build:     index.html</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Merged result: index.html  retired-guide.html  pr/123/index.html</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The preview survived, but the deleted guide did too. It could remain available at its old URL until someone explicitly removes it. The plugin&#39;s <a href="https://github.com/AgentEnder/nx-github-pages/blob/293f6bdec51acde86982c0ffb5fbc6d80e957c44/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">README</a> describes synced production deploys as merging rather than force-pushing; the published executor source still invokes <code>git push -f</code>.</p><p>This is about what the current site serves, not about erasing Git history: removing a page from the branch tip does not guarantee that previously published content is gone from history, caches, or other copies.</p><h2 id="what-we-chose" tabindex="-1">What We Chose <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-we-chose" aria-label="Permalink to “What We Chose”">​</a></h2><p>We kept the plugin for preview publishing and age-based preview cleanup. For production, <code>scripts/publish-docs.sh</code> clones the existing <code>gh-pages</code> branch, removes the old production files while preserving <code>.git</code> and <code>pr/</code>, copies in the new build, and pushes a normal commit. It rejects a production build that itself contains a top-level <code>pr</code> directory, because that path is reserved for previews.</p><p>The normal push also rejects a conflicting update instead of overwriting it. Production, preview, and cleanup jobs share a non-cancelling concurrency group, but a writer outside that group could still race; in that case the failed production deployment needs to be rerun.</p><p>A disposable Git remote test exercised the important case: two preview directories survived a production redeploy, while an old production page disappeared. Live preview comments and scheduled cleanup still need verification on GitHub.</p><h2 id="what-else-made-previews-work" tabindex="-1">What Else Made Previews Work <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-else-made-previews-work" aria-label="Permalink to “What Else Made Previews Work”">​</a></h2><p>The deployment directory is only half the problem. VitePress, diagrams, the API site, and search must all use the preview prefix when the site is built. <code>DOCS_BASE</code> supplies that prefix, and the docs, API, and combined page build cache keys include it. Otherwise an Nx cache hit could restore a production build into a preview, or the reverse.</p><p>Preview cleanup is age-based rather than PR-state-based. The plugin uses the most recent Git commit author timestamp for a preview directory, so a preview for an open PR can expire; a subsequent PR update recreates it. An unchanged deploy does not refresh its timestamp.</p><h2 id="takeaways" tabindex="-1">Takeaways <a class="header-anchor" href="#takeaways" aria-label="Permalink to “Takeaways”">​</a></h2><ul><li>A merge that preserves unrelated preview directories can also preserve deleted production files.</li><li>A shared publishing branch needs both a reserved preview path and coordinated writers.</li><li>A build-time URL prefix is part of the build&#39;s cache identity.</li><li>Check the published executor&#39;s behavior as well as its documentation before relying on deployment guarantees.</li></ul>`,24)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};